consiSt("mo",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Missouri","d":"Seven judges sitting in Jefferson City; its decisions control all other courts. Exclusive appellate jurisdiction over cases involving the validity of a U.S. treaty or statute or of a Missouri statute or constitutional provision, construction of the state revenue laws, title to a state office, and death sentences. Takes other cases by transfer from the Court of Appeals. Writes binding procedure rules, controls bar admission, and answers certified questions from federal courts (Mo. Const. art. V, §§ 2, 3, 5, 10; RSMo 477.004, 484.040)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Missouri Court of Appeals (Eastern, Western, and Southern Districts)","d":"One court in three districts: Eastern (14 judges, St. Louis region and eastern counties), Western (11 judges, all counties not assigned elsewhere, including the Kansas City area), and Southern (7 judges, southern counties). General appellate jurisdiction in everything outside the Supreme Court’s exclusive list; sits in divisions of at least three judges. An appeal filed in the wrong district or court is transferred, not dismissed (Mo. Const. art. V, §§ 3, 7, 11, 13; RSMo 477.040–.080, 477.160–.180)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts (judicial circuits of contiguous counties)","d":"Original jurisdiction over all civil and criminal cases. Each circuit has circuit judges and, in every county, at least one associate circuit judge. Circuits may adopt local rules consistent with Supreme Court rules (Mo. Const. art. V, §§ 14–17)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Associate circuit, small claims, and municipal divisions","d":"Divisions of the circuit court rather than separate courts. Some judgments of associate circuit judges in non-record cases get a trial de novo (application within 10 days); others are appealed on the record. Municipal judges hear ordinance violations. The Civil Code does not apply in small claims or municipal divisions unless made applicable (Mo. Const. art. V, § 23; RSMo 506.010, 512.180–.190)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"New Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, effective August 28, 2026","d":"Missouri repealed its narrow anti-SLAPP statute (§ 537.528) and enacted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, § 537.529 (S.B. 835 & 1111 merged with S.B. 1067, 2026). It covers claims based on communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding, communications on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding, or exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Missouri Constitution on a matter of public concern. It must be broadly construed.\nIt applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after August 28, 2026. The collected text does not say whether old § 537.528 still governs claims filed earlier.","c":"RSMo 537.529.1, .3, .13, .15; RSMo 537.528 (repealed eff. Aug. 28, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Exemptions: government defendants, enforcement actions, and commercial speech","d":"The Act does not apply to claims against a governmental unit or its employee acting officially; to claims by a governmental unit enforcing a law against an imminent threat to public health or safety; or to claims against a business arising from communications about its sale or lease of goods or services. Dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, and artistic works are not “goods or services.”","c":"RSMo 537.529.4","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: file within 60 days of service; automatic stay","d":"File a special motion to dismiss within 60 days after being served with the pleading asserting the claim (later for good cause). Filing stays all other proceedings between movant and respondent, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal expire. The court may also stay related hearings or discovery involving other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is needed and not otherwise available.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (later for allowed discovery or good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing. A fee motion, voluntary dismissal, and severance are not stayed.","c":"RSMo 537.529.5–.7, .10","v":true},{"t":"Standard: three-step test with summary-judgment evidence","d":"The court dismisses with prejudice if (1) the movant shows the Act applies, (2) the respondent fails to show an exemption, and (3) either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. The court considers pleadings, the motion and responses, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the motion; one with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"RSMo 537.529.8–.9","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","d":"The court must award costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing respondent recovers them only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"RSMo 537.529.12","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: 21 days, as of right, from a denial","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right within 21 days from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. Once any party appeals a ruling on the motion, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends.","c":"RSMo 537.529.11, .6(3)","v":true},{"t":"Old § 537.528 (pre-August 28, 2026 cases)","d":"The repealed statute applied only to actions over conduct or speech in connection with a public hearing or public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding before a state or local body. It required priority consideration of a special motion to dismiss, judgment on the pleadings, or summary judgment; suspended discovery; mandated fees if such a motion filed within 90 days of the answer was granted; and allowed an expedited appeal.","c":"RSMo 537.528 (2012 version, repealed by L. 2026 S.B. 835 & 1111 merged with S.B. 1067)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 9.03 (details not verified)","d":"Out-of-state lawyers appear in Missouri courts under Supreme Court Rule 9.03. Its requirements, including any fee, local counsel duties, and case limits, were not verified. Read the current rule on courts.mo.gov before filing.","c":"Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 9.03 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Unlicensed practice is a crime with a treble-fee remedy","d":"No one may practice law or do law business in Missouri without a Missouri license in full force. A violation is a misdemeanor, and the client may sue for treble the fees paid within two years; if the client does not, the state may. Admission and licensing are exclusively for the Supreme Court’s rules. Make sure any out-of-state appearance or advice is covered by an authorized admission.","c":"RSMo 484.020, 484.040","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances and e-filing (not verified)","d":"Missouri’s rules on limited-scope representation and on statewide electronic filing (Case.net / eFiling) are in the Supreme Court Rules, which were not collected. Statute only requires that filings omit listed personal identifiers and put them on a confidential information sheet.","c":"RSMo 509.520; Supreme Court Rules (not collected)","v":false}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Supreme Court Rule 4 (text not collected)","d":"The Missouri Rules of Professional Conduct are Supreme Court Rule 4, following the ABA Model Rules numbering (Rule 4-1.1 and so on). The rule text could not be collected, so differences from the ABA Model Rules are not stated here.","c":"Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 4 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Statutory ban on sharing fees with non-lawyers","d":"Beyond the ethics rules, a statute makes it a misdemeanor for a Missouri lawyer to divide fees with anyone who is not a licensed attorney, or with a firm, association, or corporation not wholly made up of licensed attorneys. The payor may sue for treble the amount within two years, and the state may sue if the payor does not.","c":"RSMo 484.150","v":true},{"t":"Attorney’s liens arise by statute","d":"A lawyer who appears for a party has a lien on the client’s claim or counterclaim from the start of the action, attaching to any judgment and its proceeds, that settlement cannot defeat. A contingent-fee lawyer who serves written notice of the agreement on the defendant gets a lien on the claim and settlement proceeds from the date of notice, even before suit.","c":"RSMo 484.130, 484.140","v":true}],"style":"State of Missouri","home":"https://www.courts.mo.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Tort venue is where the plaintiff was first injured","d":"If any count sounds in tort and the injury occurred in Missouri, venue lies only in the county of first injury (for defamation or privacy, where first published; for exposure, where it happened, not where symptoms appeared). Joinder cannot create venue, adding or dropping parties can force a transfer, an improper-venue motion is deemed granted if not denied within 90 days, and an erroneous denial requires reversal.","c":"RSMo 508.010.4, .8–.10, .14, .16; 507.040; 508.012"},{"t":"Never put a dollar figure in the prayer","d":"A Missouri petition seeking money may state a dollar amount only to establish jurisdiction; otherwise pray for “fair and reasonable” damages.","c":"RSMo 509.050.1"},{"t":"Punitive damages cannot be pleaded at the start","d":"An initial petition may not claim punitive damages. You need leave on a supported motion filed at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference or trial.","c":"RSMo 510.261.5"},{"t":"Medical-negligence affidavit within 90 days","d":"In any health care negligence case, file a separate affidavit for each defendant within 90 days after filing the petition (extendable once for up to 90 days for good cause), stating that you have a qualifying provider’s written opinion. Otherwise the case is dismissed on motion.","c":"RSMo 538.225.1, .4–.6"},{"t":"Two-year limits for defamation, assault, and medical malpractice","d":"Libel, slander, injurious falsehood, assault, battery, false imprisonment, and malicious prosecution must be sued within 2 years, and medical malpractice within 2 years of the act of neglect (with narrow exceptions). Most other torts and contracts have 5 years, and written promises to pay money have 10.","c":"RSMo 516.140, 516.105, 516.120, 516.110"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: 10 days after the judgment becomes final","d":"No appeal is effective unless the notice of appeal is filed within 10 days after the judgment or order becomes final. When a judgment becomes final is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84, not collected), so compute the date from the rule, not from the date of entry. A late appeal requires a special order of the appellate court.","c":"RSMo 512.050, 512.060"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days, and the appeal clock is 21","d":"In actions filed on or after August 28, 2026, a UPEPA special motion to dismiss is due 60 days after service of the pleading, and a denial must be appealed within 21 days, not the ordinary appeal period.","c":"RSMo 537.529.5, .11, .15"},{"t":"Personal-jurisdiction and venue objections are waived if not raised by motion","d":"Lack of personal jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defects are waived unless asserted by motion within the time to respond. Only failure to state a claim or defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction survive.","c":"RSMo 509.290, 509.330, 509.340, 509.400"},{"t":"Redact identifiers or use the confidential filing sheet","d":"Filings may not contain full Social Security, account, or driver’s license numbers, victim or witness contact information, a minor’s name, address, or birth date, or a party’s full birth date. Put them on the confidential information filing sheet.","c":"RSMo 509.520"},{"t":"Fee-sharing with non-lawyers is a crime","d":"Dividing fees with a non-lawyer, or a firm not wholly made up of licensed attorneys, is a misdemeanor with treble-damages exposure. This is stricter than an ethics-rule violation alone.","c":"RSMo 484.150"}],"judge":[{"t":"Peremptory change of judge: Rule 51.05 (not verified)","d":"Missouri allows a change of judge without cause by application under Supreme Court Rule 51.05. Its deadlines and limits were not verified, so read the current rule before relying on it.","c":"Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 51.05 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Statutory disqualification for cause","d":"A judge may be disqualified in a civil case for interest, prejudice, relationship to a party, prior service as counsel, or the opposing party’s undue influence. Apply by petition stating the cause and when you learned of it, with an affidavit that you cannot get a fair trial, after reasonable notice. Each side gets only one change. A judge who is interested, related, or was counsel must step aside without application unless all parties consent.","c":"RSMo 508.090, 508.100, 508.130, 508.140","v":true},{"t":"Large single-county circuits: one objection moves the case","d":"In single-county circuits over 400,000 population with two or more judges, a legal objection to the assigned judge moves the case to another division. Each party may apply once, as to one judge.","c":"RSMo 508.110","v":true},{"t":"Change of venue for local prejudice","d":"Venue may be changed if county residents are prejudiced against the applicant or the opponent unduly influences them. In counties over 65,000, a controverted application is decided on evidence. The applicant pays the costs of the change.","c":"RSMo 508.090.2, 508.140, 508.190","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Missouri Constitution: not established here","d":"The UPEPA protects rights “guaranteed by the Constitution of the United States or the Constitution of the state of Missouri.” The collected texts do not include Missouri’s Bill of Rights or case law, so whether Missouri courts read its speech guarantee more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"RSMo 537.529.3(3), .13; Mo. Const. art. I (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Supreme Court Rules override most procedure statutes","d":"The Supreme Court’s procedure rules have the force of law, and any rule may be annulled or amended only by a law limited to that purpose. Many old Civil Code timing provisions have been displaced by the Rules. The legislature has overridden Rule 51.03 for venue in tort cases.","c":"Mo. Const. art. V, § 5; RSMo 506.030, 508.011","v":true},{"t":"Punitive damages need leave of court","d":"No initial pleading may claim punitive damages. Move for leave, supported by evidence, at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference (or trial), and the court must find a reasonable basis under a clear-and-convincing standard. Damages are capped at the greater of $500,000 or five times net judgment, with exceptions, and either party may require a bifurcated trial.","c":"RSMo 510.261.5, 510.263, 510.265","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Missouri Constitution art. V (§§ 3, 5, 9–11, 13) and RSMo chapters 477, 510, 511, 512, and § 537.529, from the Revisor of Statutes (revisor.mo.gov, fetched November 10, 2026). The Missouri Supreme Court Rules on appeals (Rules 81–84, including finality, briefing, word limits, rehearing, and transfer procedure) could NOT be collected because courts.mo.gov returned HTTP 403 and prohibits automated access. Items depending on those rules are flagged; verify every rule-based deadline on courts.mo.gov. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Missouri: the statute gives you 10 days after finality, and the Rules decide when that is","d":"Most civil appeals go to one of the three districts of the Missouri Court of Appeals. The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction only over a short constitutional list (validity of statutes, revenue-law construction, title to state office, death sentences) and otherwise reviews cases by transfer.\nThe notice of appeal is due within 10 days after the judgment becomes final, a much shorter window than the federal 30 days. When a judgment becomes final, how post-trial motions extend that point, and the briefing rules are set by Supreme Court Rules 81–84, which this package could not collect. Treat the statutory items below as the floor and confirm the rules before acting."},"groups":[["Where the appeal goes",[{"t":"Court of Appeals unless the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction","d":"The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction over cases involving the validity of a U.S. treaty or statute or a Missouri statute or constitutional provision, construction of the revenue laws, title to a state office, and death sentences. The Court of Appeals has general appellate jurisdiction over everything else.","c":"Mo. Const. art. V, § 3"},{"t":"Pick the district by county","d":"The Eastern District covers the listed eastern counties and the City of St. Louis, the Southern District covers the listed southern counties, and the Western District covers every other county.","c":"RSMo 477.040–.070"},{"t":"Wrong court means transfer, not dismissal","d":"An appeal sent to the wrong district or court is transferred to the right one; want of jurisdiction is not a ground for dismissal.","c":"Mo. Const. art. V, § 11; RSMo 477.080"}]],["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final judgments and a short list of interlocutory orders","d":"An aggrieved party may appeal from a final judgment, a special order after final judgment, an order granting a new trial, an order refusing to revoke or modify an interlocutory receivership order or dissolving an injunction, and interlocutory partition judgments determining rights. Orders granting or denying class certification are appealable only if the Court of Appeals permits, and such an appeal does not stay the case unless ordered. Failing to appeal an earlier ruling does not forfeit review on appeal from the final judgment.","c":"RSMo 512.020"},{"t":"What a judgment is","d":"A “judgment” is the final determination of the parties’ rights in the action. Requirements that a judgment be denominated as such, and rules for certifying partial judgments, are in the Supreme Court Rules (not collected).","c":"RSMo 511.020"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials: immediate appeal as of right","d":"A moving party may appeal within 21 days, as of right, an order denying (in whole or part) a UPEPA special motion to dismiss. Any appeal of a ruling on the motion stays all proceedings between all parties.","c":"RSMo 537.529.11, .6(3)"},{"t":"Associate circuit cases: trial de novo or record appeal","d":"Judgments in small-claims-type and landlord-tenant cases tried before an associate circuit judge (chapters 482 and 535) and in municipal court get a trial de novo; apply with the clerk within 10 days after judgment. Bond is needed to stay execution. Other contested cases before associate circuit judges are appealed on the record like circuit cases.","c":"RSMo 512.180, 512.190"}]],["Notice of appeal",[{"t":"File in the trial court within 10 days after the judgment becomes final","d":"Appeal by filing a notice of appeal with the trial court clerk. It is not effective unless filed within 10 days after the judgment or order appealed from becomes final. Later steps are not jurisdictional, but failing to take them can lead to dismissal.","c":"RSMo 512.050"},{"t":"Contents and clerk’s duties","d":"Name the appealing parties, the judgment or order appealed from, and the appellate court. The clerk mails the notice to other parties and sends it with the docket fee to the appellate clerk; clerk errors do not affect validity.","c":"RSMo 512.070"},{"t":"Late notice only by special order","d":"Under the statute, if the ordinary time has run, the appellate court may allow a notice of appeal within six months of the final judgment by special order. You must show merit and that the delay was not due to culpable negligence. The current Supreme Court Rule may set a different period; not verified.","c":"RSMo 512.060"},{"t":"Court reporter must be paid within 10 days","d":"Transcript charges are due within 10 days after ordering the transcript, or a deposit of estimated charges within 10 days after the reporter’s written estimate. The reporter must give the estimate within 10 days of the request.","c":"RSMo 512.050"}]],["Post-trial motions and finality",[{"t":"New-trial and post-verdict motions (statutory text)","d":"Under the statute, a motion for judgment on a directed-verdict motion is due within 10 days after verdict, JNOV and arrest of judgment are abolished (raise those points in the new-trial motion), and in a bench trial a motion to amend the judgment is due within 10 days after entry. The current timing is set by Supreme Court Rule and was not verified.","c":"RSMo 510.290, 510.380, 510.310.3"},{"t":"Deemed denial at 90 days; court’s own control for 30 days","d":"A new-trial motion not ruled on within 90 days after filing is deemed denied for all purposes. The court may order a new trial on its own within 30 days after entry of judgment, and may set aside a judgment for good cause within 30 days. How these events fix the finality date that starts the 10-day appeal clock is set by Supreme Court Rule (not collected).","c":"RSMo 510.360, 510.370, 511.060"},{"t":"Preserve error in the trial court","d":"Except for subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim or defense, no error is considered on appeal unless presented to or decided by the trial court. In bench-tried cases, sufficiency of the evidence may be raised without objection below.","c":"RSMo 512.160.1, 510.310.4"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"Supersedeas bond stays execution","d":"An appeal stays execution if the appellant is a personal representative, guardian, conservator, or local government, or posts a court-approved supersedeas bond at or before filing the notice of appeal. The court may set the amount and allow up to 20 days to file it. For money judgments, the bond covers the unpaid judgment, appeal costs, interest, and delay damages unless the court sets a different amount for good cause.","c":"RSMo 512.080"},{"t":"Late bonds; $50 million cap in tort cases","d":"A late or insufficient bond may be cured in the trial court before transfer, and afterward only in the appellate court. In cases with any tort count, the total bond for all appellants may not exceed $50 million; the cap can be lifted if the appellant dissipates assets.","c":"RSMo 512.090, 512.099"}]],["Record on appeal",[{"t":"Transcript within 90 days of the notice","d":"The transcript on appeal must be filed and the case transferred to the appellate court within 90 days after the notice of appeal is filed. The trial court may extend this under 506.060. Parties may agree to an abbreviated transcript (with trial court approval) or an agreed statement of the case.","c":"RSMo 512.110, 512.120, 512.130, 512.140"}]],["Briefs and decision",[{"t":"Briefing rules come from the appellate courts","d":"Brief form, timing, number of copies, service, docketing, and argument are set by appellate court rule (Supreme Court Rule 84 and district local rules). Word limits and brief deadlines were not verified because the rules could not be collected.","c":"RSMo 512.150; Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 84 (not collected)"},{"t":"Reversal only for prejudicial error; frivolous-appeal damages","d":"Reversal requires error materially affecting the merits. The court should finally dispose of the case where possible. On affirmance or dismissal it may award the respondent damages of up to 10% of the judgment.","c":"RSMo 512.160.2–.4"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Transfer from the Court of Appeals","d":"A case goes to the Supreme Court if a dissenting judge certifies that the majority opinion conflicts with prior precedent. It may also be transferred by the Court of Appeals district after opinion, or by the Supreme Court before or after opinion, for general interest or importance, to reexamine existing law, or under Supreme Court rule. The Supreme Court then decides the case as on original appeal. Transfer application deadlines (Rule 83) were not verified.","c":"Mo. Const. art. V, § 10; Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 83 (not collected)"},{"t":"En banc transfer within the Supreme Court","d":"A case in a Supreme Court division goes en banc on an even split, by the division’s order, on the losing party’s application when a division member dissents, or under court rule.","c":"Mo. Const. art. V, § 9"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal","d":"10 days from judgment or order appealed from becomes final.\nFinality timing is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84), not collected; verify.","c":"RSMo 512.050","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (judgment or order appealed from becomes final)","note":"Finality timing is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84), not collected; verify."}},{"t":"Late notice of appeal by special order of appellate court","d":"180 days from date of final judgment.\nThe statute says six months; the current Supreme Court Rule may differ; not verified.","c":"RSMo 512.060","due":{"n":180,"from":"the trigger date (date of final judgment)","note":"The statute says six months; the current Supreme Court Rule may differ; not verified."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) special motion to dismiss","d":"21 days from order denying the motion in whole or in part.\nActions filed on or after Aug. 28, 2026.","c":"RSMo 537.529.11","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (order denying the motion in whole or in part)","note":"Actions filed on or after Aug. 28, 2026."}},{"t":"Transcript filed and case transferred to appellate court","d":"90 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nTrial court may extend under RSMo 506.060.2.","c":"RSMo 512.130","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"Trial court may extend under RSMo 506.060.2."}},{"t":"Pay court reporter for transcript","d":"10 days from ordering the transcript (or the reporter’s written estimate).","c":"RSMo 512.050","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (ordering the transcript)","note":""}},{"t":"File supersedeas bond after court sets amount","d":"20 days from order fixing bond (maximum allowed).\nBond must be presented at or before filing the notice of appeal unless time is allowed.","c":"RSMo 512.080.1(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (order fixing bond)","note":"Bond must be presented at or before filing the notice of appeal unless time is allowed."}},{"t":"New-trial motion deemed denied","d":"90 days from filing of the motion.\nStatutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule.","c":"RSMo 510.360","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion)","note":"Statutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule."}},{"t":"Court’s own order of new trial; setting aside judgment for good cause","d":"30 days from entry of judgment.\nStatutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule.","c":"RSMo 510.370, 511.060","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Statutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule."}},{"t":"Application for trial de novo (associate circuit cases under ch. 482, 535, municipal)","d":"10 days from judgment rendered.","c":"RSMo 512.190","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (judgment rendered)","note":""}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal",10,"Judgment or order appealed from becomes final","RSMo 512.050","Finality timing is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84), not collected; verify."],["Late notice of appeal by special order of appellate court",180,"Date of final judgment","RSMo 512.060","The statute says six months; the current Supreme Court Rule may differ; not verified."],["Appeal from denial of UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) special motion to dismiss",21,"Order denying the motion in whole or in part","RSMo 537.529.11","Actions filed on or after Aug. 28, 2026."],["Transcript filed and case transferred to appellate court",90,"Filing of notice of appeal","RSMo 512.130","Trial court may extend under RSMo 506.060.2."],["Pay court reporter for transcript",10,"Ordering the transcript (or the reporter’s written estimate)","RSMo 512.050",""],["File supersedeas bond after court sets amount",20,"Order fixing bond (maximum allowed)","RSMo 512.080.1(2)","Bond must be presented at or before filing the notice of appeal unless time is allowed."],["New-trial motion deemed denied",90,"Filing of the motion","RSMo 510.360","Statutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule."],["Court’s own order of new trial; setting aside judgment for good cause",30,"Entry of judgment","RSMo 510.370, 511.060","Statutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule."],["Application for trial de novo (associate circuit cases under ch. 482, 535, municipal)",10,"Judgment rendered","RSMo 512.190",""]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.courts.mo.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Missouri civil procedure statutes","rulesSrc":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"RSMo 506.010","t":"Civil Code of Missouri: scope","s":"Chapters 506–512 are “The Civil Code of Missouri” and govern civil procedure in the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, and circuit courts unless other law provides otherwise; construed for just, speedy, inexpensive determination. Does not apply to small claims or municipal divisions unless made applicable.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.020","t":"Not applicable to criminal cases","s":"The Code does not govern criminal practice except where a statute makes it applicable.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.030","t":"Supreme Court may harmonize the Code by rule","s":"If parts of the Code conflict with each other or with other procedure statutes, the Supreme Court may issue harmonizing rules that do not change substantive rights. Note Mo. Const. art. V, § 5: Supreme Court procedure rules have the force of law and may be annulled or amended only by a law limited to that purpose, so many Code sections below have been displaced by the Rules.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.040","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.050","t":"Orders and motions defined","s":"A written direction of the court not included in a judgment is an order; an application for an order is a motion.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.060","t":"Computing and extending time; motion notice","s":"Exclude the trigger day, include the last day unless a weekend or legal holiday; for periods under 7 days, exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. The court may enlarge a period before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend time for a new-trial motion, commencing an action, or taking an appeal. Written motions and hearing notice must be served at least 5 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits at least 1 day before. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.080","t":"Courts always open for filing","s":"Courts are deemed always open for filing papers, issuing and returning process, and making interlocutory motions and orders.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.090","t":"Clerk’s office hours; clerk may grant routine process","s":"The clerk’s office is open business hours except Sundays and legal holidays; the clerk may issue process and other relief not requiring a court order, subject to the court’s control.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.100","t":"Service of papers after the petition","s":"Later pleadings, written motions, notices, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every affected party (not on parties in default for failure to appear, except new claims, which need summons-type service). Serve the attorney of record unless the court orders service on the party, by hand delivery, leaving a copy at the attorney’s office, or leaving it at the party’s home with a family member over 15. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.110","t":"Commencing a suit","s":"A suit is commenced by filing a petition and either the defendant’s voluntary appearance or suing out a summons or attachment. The official annotation notes that subsection 2 (commencement requires suing out process) has been held overruled by Supreme Court Rule 53.01 (Keys v. Nigro, 913 S.W.2d 947 (Mo. App. W.D. 1996)).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.120","t":"Issuance of summons","s":"On filing the petition the clerk issues summons and delivers it for service to the sheriff or a specially appointed server; alias and pluries summons issue on request.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.130","t":"Contents of summons","s":"Signed and dated by the clerk under seal, naming the court, parties, plaintiff’s attorney, the time and place to appear and defend, and warning that default judgment will be entered for failure to do so.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.140","t":"Who serves process; special process server fees","s":"Service is by the sheriff (or coroner if the sheriff is disqualified) or a person specially appointed by the court or circuit clerk under local rules, valid only for the process named. A party may apply to have special process server fees awarded in the judgment.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.150","t":"Manner of service","s":"Summons and petition are served together: on an individual personally, at the dwelling with a family member over 15, or on an authorized agent; on a corporation, partnership, or association by delivery to an officer, partner, managing or general agent, the person in charge of a business office, or a registered agent (plus mailing if the agent statute requires). The section also covers service on conservators and dissolved corporations and notice-and-acknowledgment by mail.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.160","t":"Service by mail or publication (in rem)","s":"Allowed in actions affecting property, a fund, trust, res, or status within the court’s jurisdiction, and supports only a judgment as to that property or status, not a general personal judgment. Requires a verified petition or affidavit stating why personal service cannot be had (e.g., nonresident, absconding, concealment); the clerk mails by registered mail, return receipt signed by addressee only, or issues an order of publication.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.170","t":"Statewide service","s":"Process may be served anywhere in Missouri and forwarded to any county’s sheriff.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.180","t":"Proof of service","s":"Officers make a signed written return of time, place, and manner; special servers file an affidavit; mail service is proved by the clerk’s certificate plus the registered-mail receipt; publication by publisher’s affidavit with a copy of the notice and the clerk’s mailing certificate. No civil arrest on mesne process or execution.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.190","t":"Amending process and returns","s":"The court may allow process, return, or proof of service to be amended unless material prejudice would result.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.500","t":"Long-arm jurisdiction","s":"A person or firm submits to Missouri jurisdiction, for claims arising from the act, by transacting business, making a contract, committing a tort, owning or using real estate in Missouri, or insuring a Missouri risk (plus paternity and marital-domicile grounds).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.510","t":"Out-of-state service","s":"A defendant subject to long-arm jurisdiction may be personally served outside Missouri (a corporation through a managing officer or registered agent in any state) by a person authorized to serve there; the server files an affidavit of time, manner, and place.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 506.520","t":"Personal judgment; 30-day default bar","s":"Out-of-state service under 506.500–.510 supports a personal judgment. No default judgment may be entered until at least 30 days after service.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.010","t":"Real party in interest","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, but personal representatives, guardians, conservators, trustees of express trusts, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutory plaintiffs may sue in their own names.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.020","t":"Claims under another state’s law","s":"A claim under another state’s law may be sued on in Missouri by whoever that state authorizes, or by its authorized representative, who may receive proceeds and give a release.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.030","t":"Joinder of persons with a joint interest","s":"Persons with a joint interest must be joined on the same side; one who will not join as plaintiff may be made a defendant. The court may order other necessary parties brought in.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.040","t":"Permissive joinder (2019 limits)","s":"Parties may join if claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) and share a common question. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 20, claims arising from separate purchases of, or separate incidents involving, the same product or service do not satisfy this test, and the statute adopts State ex rel. Johnson & Johnson v. Burlison (Mo. 2019): joinder cannot extend venue to a county not proper for each claim.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.050","t":"Misjoinder; dropping and adding parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may drop, add, or sever parties at any stage. A motion to drop or add parties made with 509.290 motions is subject to 509.340’s consolidation and waiver rules.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.060","t":"Interpleader","s":"Persons with claims against the plaintiff or plaintiff’s insured exposing it to multiple liability (including multiple claims on the same insurance coverage) may be joined and required to interplead; a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.070","t":"Class and derivative actions","s":"Statutory class action provision (numerous class, adequate representation; joint/common, specific-property, or common-question rights) and shareholder derivative suits; dismissal or compromise is regulated. Class certification orders are appealable only with the Court of Appeals’ permission (RSMo 512.020(3)).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.080","t":"Third-party practice","s":"Before answering a defendant may move ex parte, and afterward on notice to the plaintiff, for leave to file a third-party petition against a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(1), leave is always needed. The third-party defendant may assert the third-party plaintiff’s defenses; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also implead.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.090","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right on timely application when a statute grants it, existing representation is or may be inadequate and the applicant may be bound, or a court-held property disposition affects the applicant; permissive intervention when a statute grants a conditional right or there is a common question, considering delay and prejudice.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.100","t":"Substitution on death, incapacity, or transfer","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, the court on motion orders substitution; the motion and hearing notice are served on parties under 506.100 and on nonparties like a summons. If the right survives only to or against remaining parties, the death is suggested on the record and the case proceeds.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.110–.188","t":"Suits by minors; next friends","s":"A minor sues only through a guardian, conservator, or court-appointed next friend; appointment procedure, bond, cost liability, and court approval of settlements and distribution of proceeds are regulated.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 507.190–.220","t":"Guardian ad litem for minor defendants","s":"A suit against a minor may not proceed until a guardian ad litem is appointed; the court appoints one if the minor fails to seek one after service.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.010","t":"Venue (2005/2019 tort venue)","s":"Non-tort cases: county of defendant’s residence, or plaintiff’s residence where defendant may be found; any county where a defendant lives; any county if all defendants are nonresidents (with independent personal jurisdiction over each). Any tort count: venue lies only in the county where the plaintiff was first injured in Missouri (defamation and privacy: where first published; trauma or exposure, not symptoms); for out-of-state injuries, the corporate defendant’s registered-agent county or the individual defendant’s residence, or plaintiff’s Missouri residence. Venue is fixed as of first injury, improper-venue motions are deemed granted if not denied within 90 days (unless waived in writing), and an erroneous denial requires reversal without showing prejudice.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.011","t":"Statute beats Rule 51.03 in tort cases","s":"To the extent Supreme Court Rule 51.03 contradicts chapter 508, the statute prevails for tort claims.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.012","t":"Transfer when parties change","s":"Before trial, if adding, removing, or severing a party would have changed venue under 508.010, the judge must transfer the case to a proper forum on any party’s application.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.013","t":"2019 venue changes: applicability","s":"The 2019 joinder and venue amendments apply to actions filed after February 13, 2019, with transition rules for then-pending cases.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.020","t":"Attachment and replevin venue","s":"Attachment and replevin suits for specific property are brought in the county where the property is found.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.030","t":"Real estate venue","s":"Suits for possession of, or affecting title to, real estate are brought in the county where the land (or part of it) lies.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.050","t":"Suits against municipalities","s":"Generally only in the county where the municipality is situated (or its seat of government if in several counties), with listed exceptions.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.060","t":"Suits against counties","s":"Brought in that county’s circuit court unless removed by change of venue.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.080","t":"Transfer by written consent","s":"Parties may agree in a filed writing to move the case to another county or court; see also 508.010.13 (court must transfer on unanimous written agreement).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.090","t":"Statutory grounds to disqualify judge or change venue","s":"A judge may be disqualified for interest, prejudice, relationship to a party, prior service as counsel, or the opposing party’s undue influence; venue may be changed for prejudice of county inhabitants or the opponent’s undue influence over them. No party gets more than one change. Peremptory change of judge is governed by Supreme Court Rule 51.05, which was not collected.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.100","t":"Self-disqualification","s":"A judge who is interested, related to a party, or was counsel must, without application, call in another judge or request a Supreme Court transfer, unless all parties consent.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.110","t":"Large single-county circuits","s":"In single-county circuits over 400,000 population with two or more judges, a legal objection to the assigned judge sends the case to another division; one application per party, as to one judge.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.130","t":"Application for disqualification or change of venue","s":"Petition stating the cause and when the applicant learned of it, with an affidavit of truth and that the applicant cannot get a fair trial.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.140","t":"Hearing on the application","s":"After reasonable notice, if the application is sufficient the judge is disqualified or venue is changed to a convenient county in the same or adjoining circuit; one change per side. In counties over 65,000, a controverted inhabitants-prejudice application is decided on evidence.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.160","t":"Pleadings closed before venue change","s":"All pleadings must be filed and issues made up before the change is ordered.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 508.190","t":"Costs of venue change","s":"The applicant pays the costs of the change (loser pays the costs of a contested inhabitants-prejudice hearing); court costs and jury expenses follow rules between the counties.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.010","t":"Pleadings allowed","s":"Petition and answer; a reply only to a counterclaim so designated; answer to a cross-claim; third-party petition and answer. The court may order a reply.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.020","t":"Caption","s":"Name of court, title of action, file number, and pleading designation; the petition names all parties.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.030","t":"Attribution and signature","s":"Pleadings of represented parties are attributed to at least one attorney of record by name with address; self-represented parties sign and give an address. No verification unless a rule or statute requires it; signing follows Supreme Court rule.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.040","t":"Simple, direct pleading","s":"Averments must be simple, concise, and direct; no technical forms.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.050","t":"Claim for relief; no dollar amount","s":"Short and plain statement of facts showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment. Unlike federal practice, a money demand may not state a dollar figure except to establish jurisdiction; pray for “fair and reasonable” damages. A party may discover the total damages claimed, but the response cannot be used at trial. Prejudgment interest under 408.040 need not be prayed.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.060","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"Any number of legal or equitable claims, independent or alternative, may be joined against an opposing party.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.070","t":"Joining contingent claims","s":"A claim normally cognizable only after another is concluded may be joined with it (e.g., a money claim and a fraudulent-conveyance claim).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.080","t":"Denials","s":"State defenses plainly and admit or deny each averment; lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial; denials must fairly meet the substance, and partial denials must specify what is admitted.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.090","t":"Affirmative defenses","s":"Must be pleaded affirmatively, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, bankruptcy discharge, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, fellow-servant injury, laches, license, payment, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, truth in defamation, waiver, and any other avoidance.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.100","t":"Effect of failure to deny","s":"Averments other than amount of damage are admitted if not denied. When a reply is filed, affirmative defenses not denied in it are admitted; averments needing no response are deemed denied.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.110","t":"Alternative and hypothetical pleading","s":"Claims and defenses may be pleaded alternatively or hypothetically; one sufficient alternative saves the pleading.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.120","t":"Numbered paragraphs; separate counts","s":"Numbered paragraphs limited to a single set of circumstances; separate counts for claims from separate transactions where it aids clarity.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.130","t":"Adoption by reference; exhibits","s":"Statements may be adopted by reference; an exhibit is part of the pleading for all purposes.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.140","t":"Capacity","s":"Capacity may be averred generally; challenge it by specific negative averment with particulars.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.150","t":"Partnership existence","s":"Pleaded partnership with named partners is confessed unless specifically denied.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.160","t":"Fraud and mistake","s":"Plead circumstances with particularity; state of mind may be averred generally.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.170","t":"Conditions precedent","s":"May be averred generally; denials must be specific and particular.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.180","t":"Official documents and acts","s":"Sufficient to aver issuance or performance in compliance with law.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.190","t":"Judgments","s":"Sufficient to aver the judgment; if controverted, the pleader must prove jurisdictional facts.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.200","t":"Special and punitive damages","s":"Items of special damage must be specifically stated. The older requirement to state punitive damages separately must now be read with 510.261.5, which bars any punitive damages claim in an initial pleading.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.210","t":"Libel and slander pleading","s":"No need to plead extrinsic facts showing application to plaintiff; defendant may plead truth and mitigating circumstances.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.220","t":"Pleading private statutes and other states’ law","s":"Cite a private statute by title and location; when the pleading states that another state’s law is relied on, courts take judicial notice of its statutes and decisions.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.230","t":"Written instruments","s":"May be pleaded by legal effect, recited at length, or attached as an exhibit.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.240","t":"Execution deemed confessed","s":"Execution of an instrument set out or attached is confessed unless specifically denied.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.250","t":"Construction","s":"Pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.260","t":"Time to plead","s":"Answer within 30 days after service of summons and petition (30 days after the return receipt is filed for mail service; 45 days after first publication). Answer a cross-claim within 20 days after it is filed; reply to a counterclaim within 20 days after the answer is filed. After a 509.290–.320 motion is denied or deferred, respond within 10 days after notice (or 10 days after a more definite statement is filed), but never less than the original time remaining. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.270","t":"Demurrers abolished","s":"Demurrers and pleas in abatement or to the jurisdiction are not used.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.280","t":"Form of motions","s":"Written unless made at a hearing or trial, stating grounds with particularity and the relief sought.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.290","t":"Defenses raised by motion","s":"Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, security for costs, lack of capacity, another action pending in Missouri, improper joinder of claims, and improper counterclaim or cross-claim; grounds may be shown by affidavit.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.300","t":"Failure to state a claim or legal defense","s":"May be raised by motion when apparent on the face of the pleadings.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.310","t":"More definite statement; bill of particulars","s":"Available when a pleading is too indefinite to respond or prepare for trial; if an order is not obeyed within 10 days after notice, the court may strike the pleading.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.320","t":"Motion to strike","s":"Redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter may be struck.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.330","t":"Time for motions","s":"Within the time to respond to the pleading or, if no response is permitted, within 20 days after service of the last pleading; motions and pleadings may be filed together without waiver.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.340","t":"Consolidation and waiver of motion defenses","s":"All available motion objections must be joined; objections not raised by motion within 509.330’s time are waived, except failure to state a claim or legal defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.350","t":"Motion searches the record","s":"A motion against an answer or reply lets the court reach earlier pleadings and rule against whoever first failed to state a claim or defense.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.360","t":"Judgment on the pleadings","s":"After pleadings close, without delaying trial.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.370","t":"Hearing motions before trial","s":"Motion objections are decided before trial on application unless deferred for good cause.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.380","t":"Hearings in chambers","s":"Motions may be heard in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing outside the county without consent.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.390","t":"Motion days; written submission","s":"Courts set regular motion times and may decide motions on written submissions without oral hearing.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.400","t":"Defenses in responsive pleading; waiver","s":"Defenses not raisable by motion go in the responsive pleading and are waived if omitted, except failure to state a claim or legal defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.410","t":"Lack of prior demand","s":"Available only if pleaded with a tender of the amount due; a plaintiff who recovers no more than the tender pays all costs.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.420","t":"Compulsory counterclaims","s":"Any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence must be pleaded, unless it is the subject of a pending action or needs parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.430","t":"Counterclaim may exceed claim","s":"A counterclaim may seek more than, or different relief from, the opposing claim.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.440","t":"After-acquired counterclaims","s":"May be added by supplemental pleading with court permission.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.450","t":"Omitted counterclaim","s":"May be added by amendment with leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect, or when justice requires.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.460","t":"Cross-claims","s":"Against a coparty for claims from the same transaction or occurrence, including indemnity or contribution.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.470","t":"Adding parties for counterclaim or cross-claim","s":"The court orders necessary additional parties brought in if jurisdiction can be obtained.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.480","t":"Assigned claims","s":"Subject to counterclaims the obligor had against the assignor at notice of the assignment (except negotiable instruments law).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.490","t":"Amendment","s":"As of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 30 days if no response is required and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise by leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond within the remaining original time or 10 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.500","t":"Amendment to conform to evidence","s":"Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded; amendment may be made any time, even after judgment.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.510","t":"Supplemental pleadings","s":"On motion, for events after the original pleading.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 509.520","t":"Redaction of personal identifiers","s":"Since August 28, 2023, filings and court records must omit full Social Security, account, and driver’s license numbers, victim and witness contact information, a minor’s name, address, and birth date, and full birth dates of parties (year allowed except for minors). The information goes on a confidential information filing sheet; family cases need a confidential case filing sheet.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.010","t":"Pretrial conference","s":"The court may hold a conference on simplifying issues, amendments, admissions, and expert limits; the resulting order controls the case unless modified to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.020","t":"Interrogatories (statutory)","s":"Answers under oath within 15 days after delivery; objections within 10 days; only one set per party without leave. The statute is much stricter than practice under the Supreme Court Rules (not collected), which govern discovery timing and limits. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.030","t":"Production and entry (statutory)","s":"On motion for good cause the court may order production of non-privileged documents and things or entry on land. A party may obtain its own previously made statement without the required showing. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.040","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause when condition is in controversy, by a physician chosen by the requesting party; the examinee gets the report free.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.050","t":"Requests for admission (statutory)","s":"After pleadings close, a party may request admission of document genuineness and related facts; matters are admitted unless a sworn specific denial or detailed explanation is served within the time designated (at least 10 days). Admissions bind only the pending action. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.060","t":"Compelling answers; sanctions","s":"Motion to compel with cost-shifting; for disobedience, orders deeming facts established, precluding evidence, striking pleadings, dismissal, or default. Costs of proving matters improperly denied may be awarded.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.070","t":"Trial docket","s":"Cases go on the trial docket as soon as possible after issue is joined.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.080","t":"Continuance","s":"For good cause, at the applicant’s cost if ordered.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.090","t":"Continuance application","s":"Written motion with affidavit, unless the adversary consents to an oral application.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.130","t":"Voluntary dismissal","s":"Plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice any time before final submission. After one dismissal and refiling, no dismissal without prejudice once the jury is sworn or evidence begins, absent stipulation or court order on affidavit. Broader than Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), which ends unilateral dismissal at the answer or summary judgment motion. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.140","t":"Involuntary dismissal","s":"For failure to prosecute or comply with the Code or orders, and at the close of plaintiff’s evidence in a bench trial.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.150","t":"Effect of dismissal","s":"Without prejudice allows refiling; with prejudice adjudicates the merits. Involuntary dismissals (except jurisdiction or venue) are with prejudice unless the order says otherwise.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.160","t":"Dismissal of counterclaims and cross-claims","s":"510.130–.150 apply to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.170","t":"Counterclaims survive dismissal","s":"Dismissing the plaintiff’s action does not dismiss a pending counterclaim or cross-claim.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.180","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Liquidated-demand suits may be consolidated; separate trials of claims or issues may be ordered for convenience or to avoid prejudice.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.190","t":"Jury right and waiver","s":"The jury right is preserved. Waiver by failing to appear, written or oral consent, or proceeding to bench trial without objection. Note the waiver-by-silence model: unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38, no written demand is required by this statute.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.200","t":"Open court","s":"Trials on the merits are in open court; hearings outside the county need consent.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.210","t":"No formal exceptions","s":"Make the requested action or objection and grounds known when the ruling is made; grounds need not be stated for objections to instructions.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.220–.230","t":"Verdicts","s":"General or special verdicts; general verdicts in money and specific-property cases.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.261","t":"Punitive damages: standard and pleading","s":"Clear and convincing proof of intentional harm without just cause or deliberate and flagrant disregard for others’ safety; limits on employer liability for agents. No initial pleading may claim punitive damages: move for leave, with supporting evidence, at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference (or trial); the court rules within 45 days. Applies to actions filed on or after August 28, 2020 (510.262).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.263","t":"Bifurcated punitive damages trial","s":"On any party’s request, the same jury decides liability and punitive liability first, then amount (net worth admissible only in stage two). Defendant may seek credit for prior punitive awards for the same conduct within the new-trial-motion time; remittitur and additur apply.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.265","t":"Punitive damages cap","s":"Greater of $500,000 or five times the net judgment, except when the state is plaintiff or the defendant pleads guilty to or is convicted of a related felony; listed housing-discrimination claims are excluded.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.270","t":"Jury assesses money recovery","s":"The jury fixes the amount and states punitive damages separately.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.280","t":"Directed verdict","s":"Replaces demurrer to the evidence; moving does not waive the right to offer evidence or the jury.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.290","t":"Post-verdict motion for judgment","s":"A party who moved for directed verdict at the close of all evidence may move within 10 days after verdict (or jury discharge) for judgment in accordance with that motion, with or without an alternative new-trial request. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.300","t":"Instructions","s":"Written instructions on request at the close of evidence; counsel may object outside the jury’s hearing; given and refused instructions are filed.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.310","t":"Bench trials","s":"On request before submission the court states grounds and, if requested, findings on controverted facts; unaddressed facts are deemed found for the result. Motion to amend the judgment within 10 days after entry. Sufficiency of evidence may be raised on appeal without a trial-court objection. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.330","t":"New trial","s":"On traditional grounds, for all or some parties and issues; only one new trial on weight of the evidence; the order must state its grounds.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.350","t":"New trial affidavits","s":"Served with the motion; opposing affidavits within 10 days, extendable up to 20 more days. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.360","t":"New trial motion deemed denied","s":"If not ruled on within 90 days after filing, the motion is deemed denied for all purposes. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.370","t":"New trial on the court’s own initiative","s":"Within 30 days after entry of judgment, stating the grounds. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.380","t":"JNOV and arrest of judgment abolished","s":"Those objections are raised in a new-trial motion or a motion filed at the same time.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 510.390","t":"Substitute judge","s":"Another judge may complete post-verdict duties or, if unable, grant a new trial.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.020","t":"Judgment defined","s":"The final determination of the parties’ rights in the action.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.030","t":"Scope of judgment","s":"For or against any of several parties, determining their rights among themselves; special protections for minors and disabled persons.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.060","t":"Setting aside within 30 days","s":"For good cause, within 30 days after entry. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.110","t":"Interlocutory default","s":"Entered against a defendant who fails to answer or plead in time.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.120","t":"Setting aside interlocutory default","s":"For good cause before damages are assessed or final judgment.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.140–.150","t":"Damages on default","s":"The court assesses liquidated damages on written instruments; otherwise plaintiff may demand a jury, or the court assesses.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.160","t":"Default relief limited to the petition","s":"Relief on default may not exceed what the petition as filed and served demanded.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.170–.190","t":"Petition for review of default","s":"A defendant not served or not appearing may petition for review for good cause; the judgment becomes absolute after 3 years.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.250","t":"Irregularity motions","s":"Must be made within 3 years after the term of the judgment.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.350–.360","t":"Judgment liens","s":"Circuit, appellate, Supreme Court, and federal judgments in Missouri are liens on the debtor’s real estate in the county (associate division judgments only after filing with the circuit clerk), for 10 years, reaching after-acquired land.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.370","t":"Revival","s":"Scire facias to revive may issue within 10 years of the judgment, not after.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.760","t":"Uniform Enforcement of Foreign Judgments","s":"Registration of sister-state and federal judgments by verified petition within the time to sue on them.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"},{"n":"RSMo 511.770–.787","t":"Uniform Foreign-Country Money Judgments Recognition","s":"Recognition of foreign-country money judgments, grounds for nonrecognition, and stays pending appeal abroad.","u":"https://revisor.mo.gov/main/ViewChapter.aspx?chapter=509"}]});
